#### HIPAA Compliance

**PHI Encryption in Cache** (HIPAA Security Rule §164.312(a)(2)(iv)):
- All PHI must be encrypted before storing in Redis cache using AES-256 encryption with keys managed in Azure Key Vault
- Encryption keys must rotate every 90 days per Gainwell security policy
- Cache entries containing PHI must include `phi=true` metadata tag for audit purposes
- Decryption only performed in application memory, never logged or persisted unencrypted

**Implementation Example**:
```csharp
public async Task<Member> GetMemberAsync(string memberId)
{
    var cacheKey = $"member:{memberId}";
    var encryptedData = await _cache.GetAsync<byte[]>(cacheKey);

    if (encryptedData != null)
    {
        var decrypted = await _encryptionService.DecryptAsync(encryptedData);
        return JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Member>(decrypted);
    }

    var member = await _database.GetMemberAsync(memberId);
    var serialized = JsonSerializer.Serialize(member);
    var encrypted = await _encryptionService.EncryptAsync(serialized);
    await _cache.SetAsync(cacheKey, encrypted, TimeSpan.FromHours(4));

    return member;
}
```

**Audit Logging** (HIPAA Security Rule §164.312(b)):
- All cache operations accessing PHI must be logged with user ID, timestamp, action (get/set/delete), and cache key
- Cache access logs must be retained for 7 years per HIPAA requirements
- Automated monitoring detects unusual cache access patterns (excessive deletions, pattern-based scans)

**TTL Limits for PHI** (HIPAA minimum necessary principle):
- PHI cache entries limited to maximum 4-hour TTL to minimize exposure window
- Sensitive PHI (SSN, payment info) limited to 15-minute TTL or not cached at all
- TTL enforcement implemented in cache service preventing longer expirations

